"Hadrons are made of quarks (§{#}). A <strong>baryon</strong> is three quarks, an <strong>antibaryon</strong> three antiquarks, and a <strong>meson</strong> a quark and an antiquark. Each quark has spin ½ and baryon number ⅓; the up, charm and top quarks have charge \\(+\\tfrac{2}{3}e\\) and the down, strange and bottom quarks \\(-\\tfrac{1}{3}e\\); the strange quark has strangeness −{#} (Table {#}). An antiquark has the opposite charge, baryon number and strangeness.",
"Each quark also carries a <strong>color</strong> charge: red, green or blue. A hadron must be color neutral: one quark of each color in a baryon, or a color and its anticolor in a meson. This is why quarks are never seen alone. Color also lets three quarks of the same flavor, like the uuu of the Δ⁺⁺, sit in the same state with their spins lined up, which the exclusion principle would otherwise forbid.",
"Hadrons with the same spin and the same light quarks (u, d, s) form families: the baryon octet (spin ½), the baryon decuplet (spin {#}/{#}) and the meson nonets. Their charges follow the Gell-Mann–Nishijima relation",
"\\[Q = I_3 + \\frac{B + S}{2}.\\]",
